    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5877",
      "problem": "Which of the following values are possible outputs of $f(x) = 2 \\sqrt{x- 2} + 2?$\n\nI. $1$\n\nII. $2$\n\nIII. $6$",
      "worked_solution": "The range of $\\sqrt x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-2} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x-2}+2 & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in [2,\\infty).$\n\nAmong the given values, only $f(x)=2$ and $f(x)=6$ lie in the range. So, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14239347/assets/10d9a611e2fbf8ebf6ae3c12b57e51ea100bf13663586379c42293d4ee45d183.png)",
